<article class="boundaries-page page container">
  <header class="boundaries-header text-center">
    <span class="hero-eyebrow">First-Timer Guidance</span>
    <h1>Comfort, Boundaries & Consent</h1>
    <p class="hero-lead" style="max-width: var(--max-paragraph); margin: 0 auto 32px; color: var(--color-espresso-soft);">
      The most important thing to know before arriving at Desire: participation is a personal choice. You can decide how much you want to join in, observe, or simply relax.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- Core Reassurance Card -->
  <div class="boundaries-hero-card">
    <h2>The Golden Principle: Participation Is a Choice</h2>
    <p>
      At both Desire Riviera Maya and Desire Pearl, couples can set their own comfort level. Whether you spend your time relaxing, joining activities, or socializing, you can decide what participation looks like for you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="boundaries-grid grid-auto-sm max-content mx-auto">
    <section class="boundary-pillar">
      <div class="pillar-icon">01</div>
      <h3>Clothing-Optional Is a Personal Choice</h3>
      <p>
        In designated clothing-optional areas, guests can choose the level of coverage that is permitted there. If you want to change your comfort level during the day, you can do so.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="boundary-pillar">
      <div class="pillar-icon">02</div>
      <h3>Watching & Observing Is an Option</h3>
      <p>
        Many first-time guests spend some time observing and getting their bearings before deciding how social they want to be. You can observe without committing to an interaction.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="boundary-pillar">
      <div class="pillar-icon">03</div>
      <h3>A Simple "No" Can Set a Clear Boundary</h3>
      <p>
        A polite <em>"Thank you, but we're just enjoying private time together"</em> is a straightforward way to communicate a boundary. If you are unsure how to respond, keep your answer simple and clear.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="boundary-pillar">
      <div class="pillar-icon">04</div>
      <h3>Clear Policy: Strict Privacy & Photography</h3>
      <p>
        Photography and recording rules are designed to protect guest privacy. Review the current published resort policy before using a phone or camera in shared areas.
      </p>
      <div class="policy-note">
        <strong>Our Concierge Advice:</strong> Treat photography as private and follow the resort's current published house rules regarding device use.
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- The Couple Alignment Section -->
  <section class="couple-alignment-box">
    <h2>Traveling as a Couple: The 3 Rules Before You Land</h2>
    <ol class="alignment-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Establish a Signal:</strong> Agree in advance on a simple way either partner can signal that they want to leave a space, return to the room, or take a breather.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Slower Partner Sets the Speed:</strong> The comfort of the partner who wants to move more slowly should guide the pace. Boundaries can change over the course of the trip.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Debrief at Breakfast:</strong> Consider checking in with each other during the trip about what you enjoyed, what you want to repeat, and what your boundaries are for the next day.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

